[[File:Pro Football Hall of Fame (23945852607).jpg|thumb|The [[Pro Football Hall of Fame]] in [[Canton, Ohio]]]]
The [[Pro Football Hall of Fame]] includes players, coaches, donors, and contributors (e.g., owners, general managers and team or league officials or other non-players) who have "made outstanding contributions to professional football". The charter class of seventeen was selected in 1963.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.profootballhof.com/hall/hof-history.aspx|title=History of the Pro Football Hall of Fame|publisher=ProFootballHallofFame.com|access-date=January 25, 2010}}</ref>

Enshrinees are selected by a 48-person selection committee which meets each year at the time and location of the [[Super Bowl]]. The rules of the committee stipulate that between four and eight individuals are selected each year.<ref name="Selection Process">{{cite web|url=https://www.profootballhof.com/hof/selectionprocess.aspx|title=Selection Process|publisher=ProFootballHallofFame.com|access-date=January 25, 2010}}</ref> Any person may nominate an individual to the hall, provided the nominee has been retired for at least five years. Not including the charter class, 76 players have been inducted in their first year of eligibility.<ref name="Hall of Famers: Yearly Finalists" >{{cite web|title=Pro Football Hall of Fame Yearly Finalists|url=https://www.profootballhof.com/hof/YearlyFinalists.aspx|access-date=March 23, 2012}}</ref>

In addition to the regular selection committee, which primarily focuses on contributions made over the past approximately thirty seasons, a nine-member seniors committee (which is a subset of the larger committee) submits two nominees each year whose contributions came prior to 1985.<ref name="Selection Process"/> These nominees are referred as "seniors nominees" (formerly "old-timer" nominees).<ref name="Senior Nominees">{{cite web | url=http://www.profootballhof.com/story/2005/1/1/senior-nominees-1972-2013/ | title=Senior Nominees, 1972&ndash;2013 | publisher=ProFootballHallofFame.com | access-date=April 28, 2014}}</ref>

In 2020, a special Blue-Ribbon Panel selected an additional 15 new members, known as the "Centennial Slate", to be inducted into the Hall of Fame to celebrate the 100th anniversary of the [[National Football League]] (NFL).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nfl.com/news/15-person-centennial-slate-for-hof-revealed-jan-15-on-nfln-0ap3000001095433|title=15-person centennial slate for HOF revealed Jan. 15 on NFLN|date=January 8, 2020|website=NFL.com}}</ref>

{{TOClimit|limit=1}}

== Inductees ==
All 32 active NFL teams have at least one player in the Hall of Fame who played for their team at some point in that player's career, with the [[Houston Texans]] ([[Andre Johnson]], class of 2024) and [[Jacksonville Jaguars]] ([[Tony Boselli]], class of 2022) being the last two teams to gain representation.<ref>{{cite web | title= Hall of Famers by Franchise | work=ProFootballHallofFame.com | url = https://www.profootballhof.com/hof/teams.aspx | access-date=January 21, 2010 }}</ref> [[Ray Guy]] was the first full-time [[Punter (football)|punter]] inducted (class of 2014), while [[Devin Hester]] was the first primary [[return specialist]] (class of 2024).<ref>{{cite web |last=Reineking |first=Jim |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/nfl/2024/02/08/devin-hester-pro-football-hall-of-fame/72529018007/ |title=Devin Hester makes history as first return specialist selected to Pro Football Hall of Fame |website=USA Today |date=February 8, 2024 |access-date=February 8, 2024}}</ref> As of 2026, both are the only honorees at their respective positions.<ref>{{cite web|title=Inductees by position {{pipe}} Punter|url=https://www.profootballhof.com/players/?pg=1&position=P|website=profootballhof.com|access-date=May 31, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Inductees by position {{pipe}} Kickoff returner|url=https://www.profootballhof.com/players/?pg=1&position=KR|website=profootballhof.com|access-date=May 31, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Inductees by position {{pipe}} Punt returner|url=https://www.profootballhof.com/players/?pg=1&position=PR|website=profootballhof.com|access-date=May 31, 2024}}</ref>

The hall is not officially affiliated with the [[National Football League]] (NFL), although, as of 2026, all but one enshrinee, [[Billy Shaw]], played in the NFL for at least a portion of his career.<ref name="Shaw">{{cite web | url=https://www.profootballhof.com/hof/member.aspx?player_id=192 | title=Billy Shaw HOF bio | publisher=ProFootballHallofFame.com | access-date=January 25, 2010}}</ref> In addition to Shaw, there are 37 other inductees who spent all or part of their careers in the [[American Football League]] that [[AFL–NFL merger|merged with the NFL]]. Shaw was the only player not to make the formal transition to the NFL, as he had retired the year before the merger.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.profootballhof.com/hof/teams.aspx|title=Players by team|publisher=ProFootballHallofFame.com|access-date=August 5, 2012}}</ref> Three players, [[Red Grange]], [[Ray Flaherty]], [[Mike Michalske]], played a portion of their career in the first [[American Football League (1926)|American Football League]] from 1926. [[Ken Strong]] is the lone Hall of Fame inductee who played during the second iteration of the [[American Football League (1936)|American Football League]] from 1936.  Strong is also one of two Hall of Famers (the other being [[Sid Luckman]]) to play in the [[American Association (American football)|American Association]]. Fifteen inductees spent some of their playing career in the [[All-America Football Conference]] during the late 1940s. Five players played at least one year in the [[Ohio League]], the predecessor to the NFL. Seven players or coaches who spent part of their careers in the original incarnation of the [[United States Football League]] (USFL) have been inducted.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.detroitsportsbroadcasters.com/blog/2009/11/02/usfl-memories-still-vivid-scott-morganroth-blog/|title=USFL Memories Still Vivid|last=Morganroth|first=Scott|date=November 2, 2009|publisher=Detroit Sports Broadcasters Association|access-date=January 21, 2010}}</ref> Two coaches (Marv Levy,<ref>{{cite web|title=Montreal Alouettes Historical Canadian Football League (CFL) Scores Since 1945 |url=http://michigan-football.com/cfl/f/montreal.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100101215221/http://michigan-football.com/cfl/f/montreal.htm |archive-date=January 1, 2010 |url-status=live |access-date=August 10, 2009 }}</ref> Bud Grant<ref name="CFHOF">{{cite web|title=Harry (Bud) Grant|url= http://www.cfhof.ca/members/harry-peter-bud-grant/|publisher=Canadian Football Hall of Fame & Museum|access-date=April 26, 2018}}</ref>), one administrator (Jim Finks), and five players (Warren Moon,<ref name="CFL">{{cite web |url=https://www.cfl.ca/index.php?module=page&id=62 |title=CFL Legends >> Warren Moon |access-date=January 22, 2007}}</ref> Fred Biletnikoff, John Henry Johnson, Don Maynard, Arnie Weinmeister) who spent part of their careers in the [[Canadian Football League]] (CFL) have been inducted. Of these inductees, three have been or are scheduled to be inducted into the [[Canadian Football Hall of Fame]]: Marv Levy, Warren Moon and Bud Grant.  Four players, [[Larry Csonka]], [[Leroy Kelly]], [[Don Maynard]], and [[Paul Warfield]], played a portion of their career in the short-lived [[World Football League]]. [[Fritz Pollard]] is the only player to play in the [[Anthracite League]]'s only season. One player ([[Kurt Warner]]) and one owner ([[Pat Bowlen]]) made significant contributions in the [[Arena Football League]]; Warner, [[Art McNally]], and [[Adam Vinatieri]] are also the only [[NFL Europe]]/[[World League of American Football|World League]] alumni in the Hall. [[Terrell Owens]] played one season of professional football in the [[Indoor Football League]] and, after his induction, played in [[Fan Controlled Football]]. [[Bill Walsh (American football coach)|Bill Walsh]] and [[Ken Stabler]] each played one season in the [[Continental Football League]].

Each entry includes the year of induction (i.e., "class"), position(s) played, team(s) each was associated with, and tenures with each team. As some inductees were both players and coaches, the position indicated here is the position indicated on the Hall of Fame website.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.profootballhof.com/hof/|title=Hall of Famers|publisher=profootballhof.com|access-date=August 5, 2012}}</ref> The Pro Football Hall of Fame website does not include CFL, USFL, WFL, indoor football leagues, and other lesser known league teams. The [[Canadian Football Hall of Fame|Canadian Football League]] and the [[Arena Football Hall of Fame|Arena Football League]] both have their own halls of fame. NFL, American Football League, All-America Football Conference, and [[Ohio League]] (listed as pre-NFL) teams are recognized in the Hall of Fame. The list is complete through the Class of 2026.

Sorting tips:
*''Sorting by position will line up players by the year they were inducted''
*Sorting by position also groups all similar positions together
**Running backs, halfbacks, fullbacks are grouped together
**Linebackers, middle linebackers, outside linebackers are grouped together
**Ends and flankers are grouped with wide receivers
**General managers and team administrators are grouped together
*Sorting by teams groups all names of a franchise together, i.e., all Cardinal teams listed under Arizona Cardinals
*When sorting, a person has a row for every team he has been with

